/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A dotted terminal is the terminal marked with a dot on a coupled coil.
   - For two coils, let i₁ and i₂ enter dotted terminals.
   - Placing the dots from the windings, when the core is drawn:
   - v₁ = L₁ di₁/dt + M di₂/dt v₂ = M di₁/dt + L₂ di₂/dt
   - Here v₁ and v₂ are the voltages of coils 1 and 2 measured from the dotted terminal to the undotted terminal; i₁ and i₂ enter those dotted terminals; L₁ and L₂ are the self-inductances; M is the mutual inductance; t is time.
   - In series, one current i flows through both coils.
   - If i enters one dot and leaves the other dot, both mutual terms become negative.
   - Leq = L₁ + L₂ + 2M (both currents enter dots) Leq = L₁ + L₂ − 2M (one enters, one leaves)
   - Here leq is the equivalent series inductance; L₁ and L₂ are the self-inductances; M is the mutual inductance.
   - In parallel, both coils have the same terminal voltage v.
*/
