/* book exam traps: The dot is a terminal mark, not a component. Moving a dot changes the sign of the mutual term, not the self-inductance value. | Dots on the same side do not always mean aiding. The actual current directions decide whether the mutual term is positive or negative. | The series mutual contribution is 2M, not M. The same current flows through both coils, so both mutual voltages appear. | Do not use the series formulas for parallel coils. In the parallel formulas the sign of 2M in the denominator reverses between aiding and opposing. | If a calculation gives M > √(L₁L₂), the coupling coefficient k is greater than 1. Check the sign, the given values, or the dot positions. */
