/* book exam traps: Turns ratio direction: n = N₂/N₁ for voltage, but the current ratio is 1/n. With the secondary current leaving the dot, I₂/I₁ = N₁/N₂. Students often invert both. | Impedance reflects by the square of the turns ratio, and the direction matters. Seen from the primary, Zin = (N₁/N₂)²ZL = ZL/n²; seen from the secondary, a primary impedance looks n² times larger. Write the direction before squaring. | The ideal transformer is the limit of a linear transformer as L₁, L₂ and M go to infinity with k = 1. Do not use the ideal ratio on a finite-L linear transformer without checking magnetizing current. | Dot reversal changes the sign of jωM in the mesh equations and the phase of V₂ by 180°. Magnitudes stay the same, so a dot question is usually about sign and phase. | Keep units: n is dimensionless, Zin and ZL are in ohms, V is in volts rms, I is in amperes rms and ω is in rad/s. */
