/* book: lead: A magnetic circuit is the path that magnetic flux takes through iron and air. A coil with N turns and current I supplies a magnetomotive force F = NI. The iron and air oppose flux with reluctance R = l/(μA). The result is Φ = F/R, the magnetic form of Ohm's law. Motors, transformers, relays, inductors and door sensors use this model. It lets you find flux from coil data and geometry before using a */
/* book Figure 4.9.1: Left: the worked example core, 500 turns carrying 2 A on iron of mean length 0.4 m with a 1 mm air gap. Right: its magnetic equivalent circuit. The gap (1.99×10⁶ A/Wb) is in series with the iron (1.59×10⁶ A/Wb), so 1000 A of MMF drives 0.279 mWb, and the 1 mm gap takes 556 A of it. */
