/* book exam traps: Air-gap reluctance uses μ₀ alone, not μ₀μr. Iron uses μ₀μr. Swapping them is the most common unit-and-physics slip. | Convert every length to metre before dividing. 1 mm = 1×10⁻³ m and 1 cm = 1×10⁻² m. Mixing mm with m² gives a reluctance off by 1000. | The analogy is not identity. MMF is not voltage, flux is not current, and reluctance is not resistance. Only the algebraic rules carry over. | In series, the same flux passes through all parts, so reluctances add. In parallel, the same MMF appears across the branches, so reluctances combine reciprocally and the fluxes add. In a three-limb core the two outer limbs are in parallel with each other and i | Saturation and fringing are ignored unless stated. Do not change μr mid-problem or add extra gap area without being told. */
